WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:00.870 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  i've always felt like climate change is  the defining issue of this generation  my passion for the environment and  conservation more broadly definitely  stem from my love of climbing  i think as a climber you wind up with a  unique perspective on climate because  you spend so much time outside up high  on the sides of cliffs looking down on  things  it gives you a vantage point that the  average tourist driving through the park  doesn't necessarily see  [Music]  my name is alex arnold i'm a  professional rock climber i've basically  spent the last 20 plus years climbing  outside all the time and you just can't  spend that much time outdoors without  starting to care about the environment  and trying to protect the wild places  that you climate  [Music]  they're the basic things as a climber  like leaving no trace making sure you  don't make a mess of the crag try not to  create the environment more than you  have to really i think the climbing  lifestyle is relatively low impact  except that you're constantly traveling  between different climbing areas if you  go to an area stay there for at least a  [Music]  month i don't think there was one  specific moment as a climber that made  me realize that the climate was changing  i've traveled fairly widely i've just  seen quite a bit and so have a lot of  different examples from different parts  of the world the polar regions in  particular but also just the high  mountains with glaciated peaks are  really where the world is changing the  fastest when you look at these peaks  that seem permanent and then you see  that the glaciers are just retreating so  quickly that it changes the way you  interact with the mountains and that  just strikes you as wrong you normally  think of those types of landscapes as  sort of transcending human time frames  and yet just in the time that i've been  a climber i've seen them change  drastically  so i went to eat somebody a bunch as a  kid  but then my first time climbing there i  was 18 or 19.  you know i've been climbing yosemite for  15 years  and now it's unimaginably hot to climb  there in june or september the  comfortable climbing season has shifted  months on either side it is slightly  hard to plan for as a climber now you  should just anticipate that any kind of  ice route may or may not be in and you  should know that when you hike into the  mountains like that there's a good  chance that it's just not going to be in  condition things are changing pretty  quickly and you just don't totally know  as a result of my travel experience as a  climber and seeing how quickly the world  is changing i started the haunted  foundation which supports solar projects  around the world  so sometimes that means solar panels on  a school for indigenous girls in  guatemala with solar-powered boats in  the amazon grid tied solar systems in  the u.s in low income communities and  basically all kinds of different systems  i think that  it's hard for people that live in cities  to appreciate how quickly  things are changing in the more  remote and rugged places on earth for me  personally as as a climber and as an  athlete  i like to show  the natural surroundings that i'm  climbing in and so edge of the unknown  is a new national geographic series  showing  how top athletes do what they do  and it allows folks at home an  opportunity to see some of the edges of  earth that you're just not seeing  normally  in my case my episode is sort of a  deeper dive into this experience we had  climbing walls in morocco and it's  exactly the kind of place that is most  affected by climate particularly because  it's north africa and so it's already  climatically extreme cold in winter hot  in the summer it's up high these are the  types of communities that are most  affected by climate change and the  people who are at least able to adapt  when you read statistics about climate  refugees these are the communities that  you're reading about i hope that anyone  watching edge of the known comes away a  little bit inspired about  how we do the sports that we do and the  hard work that goes into performing at  this level but i hope that they're also  inspired by the natural beauty of the  world and the locations in which we're  doing these sports  when i think about the world that my  child will eventually inhabit i prefer  to think that it could actually be great  if humanity chooses to rise to the  occasion and meet some of these  challenges we just haven't quite chosen  to take that path yet but the optimist  in me just feels like you may as well at  least think about the ways in which you  can improve the world by acting on  climate and i think that's the more  tenable path forward  [Music]  [Applause]

